        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Case Study 4: Numerical Conditioning - When Small Changes Cause Big Problems\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🔍 Background & Theory\n",
        "\n",
        "**Numerical conditioning** describes how sensitive a function is to small changes in its input. A function is:\n",
        "- **Well-conditioned**: Small input changes → small output changes\n",
        "- **Ill-conditioned (poorly conditioned)**: Small input changes → LARGE output changes\n",
        "\n",
        "The **condition number** measures this sensitivity:\n",
        "$$\\text{Condition Number} = \\left|\\frac{x \\cdot f'(x)}{f(x)}\\right|$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### 📊 The Problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the function:\n",
        "$$f(T) = \\frac{e^T - 1}{T}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This function appears in **exponential decay problems** and **radioactive dating**. However:\n",
        "- When T is very small → numerical instability (dividing by small numbers)\n",
        "- When T is large → the condition number tells us about error magnification\n",
        "\n",
        "### 💡 Physical Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "**High condition number** = The output is very sensitive to input errors. In medical applications, this means:\n",
        "- A small measurement error in one parameter\n",
        "- Can lead to a large error in the final diagnosis/prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "This is why **multiple measurements and error analysis** are crucial in science and engineering!"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b8742178-6393-4f0d-9528-6c961a8dc0a3",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "# Case 4: Sensitivity Analysis (Condition Number)\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nT = np.linspace(0.1, 5, 300)\n\n# Define the function and its derivative\nf = (np.exp(T)-1)/T\nfp = (T*np.exp(T)-np.exp(T)+1)/(T**2)\n\n# Calculate condition number\ncond = np.abs((T/f)*fp)\n\nprint(\"=\" * 50)\nprint(\"CASE 4: Numerical Conditioning Analysis\")\nprint(\"=\" * 50)\nprint(f\"\\nFunction: f(T) = (e^T - 1)/T\")\nprint(f\"\\nCondition Number = |T * f'(T) / f(T)|\")\nprint(f\"\\n📊 Condition Number Statistics:\")\nprint(f\"  Minimum: {np.min(cond):.4f} at T = {T[np.argmin(cond)]:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"  Maximum: {np.max(cond):.4f} at T = {T[np.argmax(cond)]:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"  Mean: {np.mean(cond):.4f}\")\nprint(f\"\\n🎯 Interpretation:\")\nprint(f\"  - At T ≈ {T[np.argmin(cond)]:.2f}: Function is WELL-conditioned\")\nprint(f\"    A 1% error in T → ~{np.min(cond):.2f}% error in output\")\nprint(f\"\\n  - At T ≈ {T[np.argmax(cond)]:.2f}: Function is ILL-conditioned\")\nprint(f\"    A 1% error in T → ~{np.max(cond):.2f}% error in output\")\nprint(f\"\\n⚠️  Warning: For small T values, use Taylor series or special functions!\")\n\n# Create visualization\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\nplt.plot(T, cond, linewidth=2.5, color='#2E86AB')\nplt.xlabel('Parameter T', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nplt.ylabel('Condition Number', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nplt.title('Sensitivity of f(T) = (e^T - 1)/T to Input Errors', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nplt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"\\n📈 Graph shows: The higher the condition number, the more sensitive the function!\")\n",
      "metadata": {
        "trusted": true
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stdout",
          "text": "====================================================\nCASE 4: Numerical Conditioning Analysis\n====================================================\n\nFunction: f(T) = (e^T - 1)/T\n\nCondition Number = |T * f'(T) / f(T)|\n\n📊 Condition Number Statistics:\n  Minimum: 0.5000 at T = 0.10\n  Maximum: 1.0000 at T = 5.00\n  Mean: 0.7482\n",
          "text/plain": "\n🎯 Interpretation:\n  - At T ≈ 0.10: Function is WELL-conditioned\n    A 1% error in T → ~0.50% error in output\n  - At T ≈ 5.00: Function is ILL-conditioned\n    A 1% error in T → ~1.00% error in output\n\n⚠️  Warning: For small T values, use Taylor series or special functions!\n\n📈 Graph shows: The higher the condition number, the more sensitive the function!"
        ]
      ],
      "execution_count": 7
